export const RIVERSIDE_REVIEW_HTML = "<!doctype html><html lang=\"en-AU\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1\"><meta name=\"robots\" content=\"noindex,nofollow\"><title>Gentle Care | Five locality drafts for review</title><style>*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#f3f6f5;color:#183c43;font:17px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif}main{max-width:1040px;margin:auto;padding:32px 20px}h1,h2,h3{line-height:1.2}h1{font-size:clamp(30px,5vw,46px)}h2{font-size:28px}a{color:#12616b;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.eyebrow{font-size:12px;font-weight:750;letter-spacing:.12em}nav{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px}nav a{padding:8px 12px;background:white;border:1px solid #cbdad7;border-radius:7px}.notice,.status{background:#fff3d5;padding:18px;border-left:4px solid #a66b12}.notice strong{display:block}article{background:white;border:1px solid #d6e1de;border-radius:14px;padding:26px;margin:24px 0}summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;padding:16px;background:#e8f0ee;border-radius:6px}details[open] summary{margin-bottom:22px}.draft h2{font-size:24px}.draft h3{font-size:20px}.draft section{margin:25px 0}.draft aside{background:#fff3d5;padding:16px}.decision{padding:16px;background:#edf3f2;margin:20px 0}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere}@media print{body{background:white}article{break-before:page;border:0;padding:0}nav,summary{display:none}.draft{display:block}details:not([open])>*:not(summary){display:block!important}}</style></head><body><main id=\"top\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">GENTLE CARE NURSING \u00b7 13 NOVEMBER 2026</p><h1>Five locality drafts for your review</h1><p>Liberty Grove, Sydney Olympic Park, Lidcombe, Newington and Wentworth Point. One file, five complete drafts, with local-source limits kept visible.</p><div class=\"notice\"><strong>Drafts only. Nothing here is published or approved for production.</strong>These are not the five PR79 pages. Earlier approval does not transfer to this batch. Clinical, funding, staffing, intake and service descriptions retained from the original drafts still require review. A yes to wording is not a clinical sign-off or a deployment instruction.</div><h2>How to review</h2><ol><li>Open each full draft below. Read the yellow evidence note first.</li><li>Mark yes, no or changes needed for each page. Note any inaccurate local details.</li><li>Gemma confirms the actual clinical and service claims. Resolve flagged source facts before release.</li></ol><p>Four drafts have one local-evidence paragraph revised. Wentworth Point is the recovered original and remains on an evidence-refresh hold. No new clinical claims were added in this pass.</p><nav aria-label=\"Locality drafts\"><a href=\"#liberty-grove\">Liberty Grove</a><a href=\"#sydney-olympic-park\">Sydney Olympic Park</a><a href=\"#lidcombe\">Lidcombe</a><a href=\"#newington\">Newington</a><a href=\"#wentworth-point\">Wentworth Point</a></nav><article id=\"liberty-grove\"><header><p class=\"eyebrow\">NEXT BATCH \u00b7 REVIEW ONLY</p><h2>Liberty Grove</h2><p class=\"status\">Vehicle entrances and pedestrian connections are distinct. Local evidence was checked on 12 November; property-specific access is not verified.</p></header><details open><summary>Read the full Liberty Grove draft</summary><div class=\"draft\"><aside><strong>13 November evidence-only revision. NOT APPROVED FOR PUBLICATION.</strong> Source observations below are dated 12 November, not a fresh 13 November check. Clinical and service text is preserved but remains unapproved for this batch. PR79 approval does not apply.</aside><nav>Review index</nav><h2>Prepare home care and nursing in Liberty Grove</h2><aside>Unpublished draft for Gemma. Editing and evidence review are separate from release approval.</aside><p>Discuss care in Liberty Grove with clear household entry, visitor parking, privacy and support responsibilities suited to the actual home and requested tasks.</p><p>For support in Liberty Grove, consider the person&#39;s care needs and the practical steps involved in reaching their home. A clear entry plan helps keep the discussion focused on tasks, daily preferences and chosen participants. Gentle Care can consider your enquiry while household and service arrangements are checked.</p><section><h2>Confirm the care location in Liberty Grove</h2><p>The coverage register identifies Liberty Grove, NSW, with recorded postcode 2138. Confirm the actual suburb and postcode for the address where care is needed, particularly when a relative, referrer or billing contact lives elsewhere. The recorded postcode is locality context, not verification of every street address.</p><p>Gentle Care&#39;s coverage for Liberty Grove is owner-reported confirmed as recorded on 2026-11-05. The requested tasks, suitable staff, funding and visit times still need to be agreed for your enquiry. Coverage does not confirm an appointment or establish an office in this locality.</p></section><section><h2>Plan the proposed visit in Liberty Grove</h2><p>Give the suburb, general care need and preferred start date first. Explain any essential visit times, current providers and whether a move or hospital discharge affects the timing. A proposed discharge or transition date does not reserve a visit.</p><p>Ask how the exact service address and access instructions should be shared securely. Discuss any stairs, shared entrance, equipment or privacy needs at the individual property; these are questions about your home, not assumptions about housing in the locality. Confirm any travel charges, cancellation terms and who to contact if plans change.</p><ul><li>Where will care actually take place, and who may help coordinate it?</li><li>What tasks and timing need to be assessed before a first visit?</li><li>Who holds the current instructions and can share them with consent?</li><li>What must be confirmed before you rely on a start date?</li></ul></section><section><h2>Describe your home within Liberty Grove</h2><p>The Liberty Grove Community Association describes a mix of apartment buildings, townhouses, duplexes and freestanding homes within its community scheme. Give Liberty Grove as the service suburb, then confirm the specific street, dwelling and entrance during intake. The estate name alone cannot identify a building, unit or the route a visitor should take.</p><p>Describe the actual entrance, including any lobby, intercom, lift, steps or gates. Ask the household to verify these details rather than assuming access features from the property title or shared facilities. Mention a temporary stay or planned move early.</p></section><section><h2>Check the visitor route and parking with the household</h2><p>The Community Association distinguishes vehicle entrances from pedestrian-only connections. When planning a visit, identify the vehicle approach and the route from the street to the actual dwelling, rather than relying on a pedestrian connection shown on a map. Confirm property-specific visitor parking and entry arrangements with the household. A local access map does not grant permission to enter a building or guarantee an available parking space.</p><p>The same source sets out parking restrictions, including no parking in Private Access Ways. Do not treat a paved access lane as a place to leave a vehicle or assume a visitor space will be free. Identify a permitted stopping or parking arrangement and the onward route to the dwelling before relying on it. Local parking information does not grant access to a home, shared building or resident-only facility.</p></section><section><h2>Keep entry arrangements separate from permission to discuss care</h2><p>Name the entry contact and agree what happens if a visitor cannot get in. Ask how necessary access details should be shared securely. Building staff or another resident helping with entry are not automatically authorised to receive personal information or make care decisions.</p><p>The person receiving support should be involved in deciding who attends the first discussion and what may be shared. Explain communication preferences and any authorised representative&#39;s role. In a shared household, consider where a private discussion and the proposed care could take place. Do not send keys, codes, health records or another resident&#39;s details through a general website enquiry.</p></section><section><h2>Fit the proposed support around the household routine</h2><p>Describe the nursing or everyday assistance needed, preferred frequency and anything important about when the person is at home. Explain the support already provided by family, other workers or a SIL team where relevant. A visiting nursing arrangement should make clear which tasks remain with the existing team and who can authorise updates to care instructions.</p><p>Explain the proposed NDIS, DVA, older-person care or private arrangement and ask which requirements apply. Confirm suitable staffing, equipment, supplies, rates and travel or cancellation charges before agreeing a start. For a lengthy visit, check whether additional visitor-parking arrangements are needed.</p></section><section><h2>Start with a brief summary of the care you need</h2><p>Start with Liberty Grove, the general support wanted and a preferred contact method. Mention a planned discharge, provider change or entry concern. Ask what is needed to assess the request and how to prepare without sharing unnecessary personal information.</p><p>Keep your first website message to your suburb, the general support you need and contact details. Ask how to share care plans and access instructions securely. Do not put health records, participant identifiers, keys or door codes in the enquiry box.</p><p>This enquiry is for planned support. If someone has a medical emergency or is in immediate danger, call 000 rather than waiting for a website reply.</p></section><h2>Referral preparation</h2><ul><li>Liberty Grove service location and the dwelling or building entrance to confirm during intake.</li><li>General support tasks, routine, preferred frequency and any planned changeover date.</li><li>Household contact who can verify current entry and visitor-parking arrangements.</li><li>The person&#39;s communication preferences, consent and chosen participants in care discussions.</li><li>Existing providers, current instructions and questions about equipment, funding and charges.</li></ul><h2>Process</h2><ol><li><b>Describe the support</b> Explain the person&#39;s priorities and the tasks needed at the Liberty Grove home.</li><li><b>Confirm household arrangements</b> Check the exact entrance, visitor route, parking and who can provide entry.</li><li><b>Review care responsibilities</b> Discuss consent, current providers, instructions, suitability, funding and fees.</li><li><b>Agree the first visit</b> Confirm timing and the contact for access problems once the service requirements are settled.</li></ol><h2>Questions</h2><h3>Does coverage in Liberty Grove guarantee a visit?</h3><p>No. Owner-confirmed coverage supports making an enquiry, but the team must confirm the proposed tasks, staffing, funding and visit arrangement for the individual person.</p><h3>Which location details should I give for care in Liberty Grove?</h3><p>Start with Liberty Grove and confirm the postcode for the care address; the register records 2138. Distinguish it from a family or billing address. Ask how to share the exact address and necessary access information securely.</p><h3>Does this page identify an office or travel time in Liberty Grove?</h3><p>No. The locality inventory uses geographic boundaries and straight-line measurements. These do not establish an office, driving route, travel time or an appointment response time.</p><h3>Are Liberty Grove&#39;s estate roads ordinary council roads?</h3><p>The Community Association describes the estate roads and pathways as community-maintained and distinguishes Open Access Ways from Private Access Ways. Check current visitor arrangements with the household or appropriate property contact before planning a visit.</p><h3>Can a visiting worker park in a private access lane?</h3><p>The Association&#39;s published parking information prohibits parking in Private Access Ways. Confirm a permitted parking arrangement for the actual visit. This page cannot reserve a visitor bay or grant an exception.</p><h3>Does estate access mean a worker can enter my building?</h3><p>Entry to your dwelling and any shared building must be arranged separately. Confirm who can provide access and how necessary instructions will be shared securely. Publicly available estate information is not permission to enter private property.</p><h3>Can building staff discuss my care on my behalf?</h3><p>Only with the appropriate permission or decision-making authority. Someone helping a visitor through an entrance is not automatically entitled to receive health information. Make the person&#39;s wishes and authorised contacts clear.</p><h3>Can nursing be arranged alongside an existing SIL team?</h3><p>Describe the nursing task and the house team&#39;s current responsibilities. Suitability, funding, instructions and handover need agreement. A nursing enquiry does not establish accommodation availability or continuous staffing.</p><aside><h2>Review questions</h2><ul><li>Gemma: confirm which visiting nursing and everyday support tasks can be considered in Liberty Grove.</li><li>Who checks the household entry plan, equipment needs and any parking concern before a first visit?</li><li>Confirm the secure handling of access details and separation of entry contacts from authorised care contacts.</li><li>Verify funding, written fees and responsibilities where family or another provider remains involved.</li><li>Recheck the Community Association&#39;s current guidance before publication; do not imply entry rights, reserved parking, resident-facility access or an estate partnership.</li><li>Gemma must review the actual service scope, referral process and capacity for the proposed arrangement.</li><li>Funding, clinical suitability and applicable credential scope require their own checks; no clinical sign-off is assigned by composition.</li><li>Distinct useful local evidence is not established by suburb/postcode substitution or shared prose. Resolve local value and canonical overlap before considering indexing.</li><li>Existing routes, metadata and technical QA require review and explicit release approval before publication.</li></ul><h2>Evidence</h2><ul><li>Owner-reported coverage: the registry records Tyson&#39;s report of Gemma&#39;s confirmation on 5 November 2026. This establishes the locality planning scope only, not accepted clinical tasks, funding eligibility, an available appointment or clinical approval.</li><li>Registry identity checked 6 November 2026: Liberty Grove, NSW 2138, nsw-13034; owner-reported-confirmed coverage; existingPath is null. This record does not grant estate or property access.</li><li>Read 6 November 2026: Liberty Grove Community Association, Living in Liberty Grove, https://www.libertygrove.info/about-liberty-grove/facilities-1 . Supports the mix of apartments and other dwelling types and community-scheme context. Does not establish access features or visitor permissions for an individual property.</li><li>Read 6 November 2026: Liberty Grove Community Association, Roads and Parking, https://www.libertygrove.info/about-liberty-grove/roads-and-parking . Supports community maintenance, Open/Private Access Ways, shared road use and the prohibition on parking in Private Access Ways. Published rules require current property-level checking; no enforcement practice, speed limit or bay availability is asserted.</li><li>Evidence gaps: no property assessment, access permission, estate relationship, reserved parking, local roster, clinical sign-off or entitlement to resident-only facilities verified. Care-planning suggestions are distinct from the Association&#39;s factual descriptions.</li><li>Locality identity: NSW Spatial Services gazetted Suburb layer, object ID 13034; https://portal.spatial.nsw.gov.au/server/rest/services/NSW_Administrative_Boundaries_Theme_multiCRS/FeatureServer/2. Postcode comes from the coverage register and must be checked for the actual address.</li><li>Coverage is owner-reported confirmed, recorded 2026-11-05; it is not independent verification of clinical scope or credentials.</li><li>Straight-line geographic inventory, not driving distance, travel time, an office-coordinate geocode, or a service availability guarantee. No office or travel time is inferred.</li><li>Shared content composition is not independent local research, approval or sufficient evidence for a separately indexed page.</li></ul></aside></div></details><div class=\"decision\"><strong>Decision for Liberty Grove</strong><p>Copy: Yes / No / Changes needed &nbsp; \u00b7 &nbsp; Local facts: Confirmed / Needs checking &nbsp; \u00b7 &nbsp; Clinical and service review: Pending</p><p>Notes: _________________________________________________</p></div><a href=\"#top\">Back to overview</a></article><article id=\"sydney-olympic-park\"><header><p class=\"eyebrow\">NEXT BATCH \u00b7 REVIEW ONLY</p><h2>Sydney Olympic Park</h2><p class=\"status\">A car-park booking does not reserve an individual accessible bay. Check current conditions for the relevant date; no journey or appointment guarantee.</p></header><details open><summary>Read the full Sydney Olympic Park draft</summary><div class=\"draft\"><aside><strong>13 November evidence-only revision. NOT APPROVED FOR PUBLICATION.</strong> Source observations below are dated 12 November, not a fresh 13 November check. Clinical and service text is preserved but remains unapproved for this batch. PR79 approval does not apply.</aside><nav>Review index</nav><h2>Plan home nursing and care in Sydney Olympic Park</h2><aside>Unpublished draft for Gemma. Editing and evidence review are separate from release approval.</aside><p>Arrange a Sydney Olympic Park care enquiry around the actual home, event-day access, family travel and clear support, funding and handover requirements.</p><p>Care at a Sydney Olympic Park home needs to fit the person&#39;s routine and the arrangements for reaching the building. Describe the tasks and any important timing. Gentle Care can consider the request while you clarify access, existing providers and what must be agreed before visits begin.</p><section><h2>Confirm the care location in Sydney Olympic Park</h2><p>The coverage register identifies Sydney Olympic Park, NSW, with recorded postcode 2127. Confirm the actual suburb and postcode for the address where care is needed, particularly when a relative, referrer or billing contact lives elsewhere. The recorded postcode is locality context, not verification of every street address.</p><p>Gentle Care&#39;s coverage for Sydney Olympic Park is owner-reported confirmed as recorded on 2026-11-05. The requested tasks, suitable staff, funding and visit times still need to be agreed for your enquiry. Coverage does not confirm an appointment or establish an office in this locality.</p></section><section><h2>Plan the proposed visit in Sydney Olympic Park</h2><p>Give the suburb, general care need and preferred start date first. Explain any essential visit times, current providers and whether a move or hospital discharge affects the timing. A proposed discharge or transition date does not reserve a visit.</p><p>Ask how the exact service address and access instructions should be shared securely. Discuss any stairs, shared entrance, equipment or privacy needs at the individual property; these are questions about your home, not assumptions about housing in the locality. Confirm any travel charges, cancellation terms and who to contact if plans change.</p><ul><li>Where will care actually take place, and who may help coordinate it?</li><li>What tasks and timing need to be assessed before a first visit?</li><li>Who holds the current instructions and can share them with consent?</li><li>What must be confirmed before you rely on a start date?</li></ul></section><section><h2>Identify the home rather than a venue meeting point</h2><p>Confirm the residential service address, entrance and who will be at home. A stadium, station or precinct name cannot identify the correct lobby or dwelling. If a relative enquires from elsewhere, keep their address separate from the address where care is needed.</p><p>Ask the resident or appropriate building contact about visitor entry, any lift arrangements and permitted parking. Describe the actual route from the entrance to the care space, including any known equipment-access concern. Sydney Olympic Park&#39;s public facilities cannot establish the accessibility of an individual dwelling or give a visiting provider permission to enter it.</p></section><section><h2>Review access for the date of the proposed visit</h2><p>Sydney Olympic Park&#39;s parking guidance says major events can affect roads and on-street parking, and directs residents and visitors to date-specific road-closure information. For a recurring care arrangement, check the relevant date rather than assuming the route used last time will remain available. Ask the building contact whether any current instructions affect the entrance you normally use.</p><p>Explain essential care timing for the provider to assess. Agree who reports an access problem, how you will be contacted and what happens if plans change. Event notices cannot establish a worker&#39;s journey time, and Gentle Care&#39;s ability to meet the requested timing needs confirmation.</p></section><section><h2>Separate building parking from public transport planning</h2><p>Sydney Olympic Park\u2019s accessible-parking guidance distinguishes booking a car park from reserving an individual accessible bay: a booking does not reserve that specific space. Check current information for the destination and date, and confirm residential visitor arrangements separately with the building contact. Public parking information does not establish accessibility of the onward route to a private home.</p><p>For a relative joining you, the Authority&#39;s travel page distinguishes Olympic Park Station from Sydney Olympic Park Ferry Wharf and describes a connecting bus for the wharf. Choose the arrival point deliberately and check the complete journey to the home, including accessibility and current services. Do not assume the shared Olympic Park name means the station, wharf and residential destination are interchangeable.</p></section><section><h2>Keep responsibilities clear when plans change</h2><p>Describe the tasks, routine and existing support. Explain any appointments away from home, expected return and handover from another worker. Discuss outings, venue attendance or transport separately so responsibilities are clear before assuming they are included in a home visit.</p><p>For a discharge or provider transition, identify who holds current care instructions and who may discuss them with consent. Ask how suitability, staff availability and funding will be checked under the relevant NDIS, DVA, older-person care or private arrangement. Confirm the accepted work, equipment responsibilities, costs and travel or cancellation terms before changing existing support. Keep a proposed start date distinct from an agreed appointment.</p></section><section><h2>Make the first enquiry useful and private</h2><p>Start with Sydney Olympic Park, the general help wanted and your preferred contact method. Mention building-access issues or a proposed transition date. Say who the person wants involved and whether their attendance depends on travel, then ask what information and checks are needed next.</p><p>Keep your first website message to your suburb, the general support you need and contact details. Ask how to share care plans and access instructions securely. Do not put health records, participant identifiers, keys or door codes in the enquiry box.</p><p>This enquiry is for planned support. If someone has a medical emergency or is in immediate danger, call 000 rather than waiting for a website reply.</p></section><h2>Referral preparation</h2><ul><li>Sydney Olympic Park service address and the correct residential entrance to confirm during intake.</li><li>Care tasks, preferred routine and any essential timing requiring assessment.</li><li>Current building-contact information for entry and visitor parking, shared only as appropriate.</li><li>Known event or works notices relevant to the proposed date, checked against current information.</li><li>Existing providers, authorised participants, current instructions and funding questions.</li></ul><h2>Process</h2><ol><li><b>Explain the home-care need</b> Describe the support requested, the service location and the person&#39;s priorities.</li><li><b>Check the visit context</b> Clarify the entrance, parking, relevant access notices and chosen family attendance.</li><li><b>Review the care arrangement</b> Confirm suitability, instructions, funding, charges and responsibilities with existing providers.</li><li><b>Agree timing and communication</b> Set the visit details and the contact plan for changed access or household circumstances.</li></ol><h2>Questions</h2><h3>Does coverage in Sydney Olympic Park guarantee a visit?</h3><p>No. Owner-confirmed coverage supports making an enquiry, but the team must confirm the proposed tasks, staffing, funding and visit arrangement for the individual person.</p><h3>Which location details should I give for care in Sydney Olympic Park?</h3><p>Start with Sydney Olympic Park and confirm the postcode for the care address; the register records 2127. Distinguish it from a family or billing address. Ask how to share the exact address and necessary access information securely.</p><h3>Does this page identify an office or travel time in Sydney Olympic Park?</h3><p>No. The locality inventory uses geographic boundaries and straight-line measurements. These do not establish an office, driving route, travel time or an appointment response time.</p><h3>Can major events affect a home-care visit?</h3><p>The Authority says events can affect roads and parking. Check current notices for the proposed date and confirm the building entrance. Discuss essential care timing and the contact plan rather than assuming a route or appointment will be unaffected.</p><h3>Is visitor parking guaranteed at my building?</h3><p>No availability is established by this page. Ask the resident or building manager about the actual visitor arrangements and permitted alternatives. Public parking information does not reserve a bay or authorise access to a private garage.</p><h3>Are Olympic Park Station and the ferry wharf the same arrival point?</h3><p>No. The Authority&#39;s travel information describes them separately and identifies a bus connection for the wharf. Check the complete journey to the residential address, including any accessibility needs and current service changes.</p><h3>Can a home visit include help attending an event?</h3><p>Describe event attendance, travel or community assistance as a separate request. Tasks, staffing, suitability, funding and any venue requirements must be checked before assuming this is included in home support.</p><h3>What should I do if building access changes?</h3><p>Use the contact arrangement agreed with the provider and explain what has changed. Confirm any revised entrance or visit plan. Do not send access codes through a general website form or assume a public road notice updates the care team automatically.</p><aside><h2>Review questions</h2><ul><li>Gemma: which nursing and everyday support tasks can be considered for Sydney Olympic Park homes?</li><li>Confirm how visit timing, access disruptions and changes to an agreed appointment are handled.</li><li>Who checks building entry, parking, equipment access and any travel charges?</li><li>Verify funding checks, consent, existing-provider handover and the secure referral process.</li><li>Recheck the Authority&#39;s current access guidance before publication; do not imply event-proof travel, reserved parking, transport provision, venue affiliation or public routes.</li><li>Gemma must review the actual service scope, referral process and capacity for the proposed arrangement.</li><li>Funding, clinical suitability and applicable credential scope require their own checks; no clinical sign-off is assigned by composition.</li><li>Distinct useful local evidence is not established by suburb/postcode substitution or shared prose. Resolve local value and canonical overlap before considering indexing.</li><li>Existing routes, metadata and technical QA require review and explicit release approval before publication.</li></ul><h2>Evidence</h2><ul><li>Owner-reported coverage: the registry records Tyson&#39;s report of Gemma&#39;s confirmation on 5 November 2026. This establishes the locality planning scope only, not accepted clinical tasks, funding eligibility, an available appointment or clinical approval.</li><li>Registry identity checked 6 November 2026: Sydney Olympic Park, NSW 2127, nsw-24217; owner-reported-confirmed coverage; existingPath is null. Proposed path remains an unpublished candidate.</li><li>Read 6 November 2026: Sydney Olympic Park Authority, Parking FAQs, https://www.sydneyolympicpark.nsw.gov.au/parking-faqs . Supports event-related road and parking changes, date-specific closure guidance, marked-bay restrictions and checking visitor parking with a building manager. This is not a live access check, parking reservation or assessment of a particular building.</li><li>Read 6 November 2026: Sydney Olympic Park Authority, How to get here, https://www.sydneyolympicpark.nsw.gov.au/visiting-the-park/public-transport . Supports distinct station/wharf arrival planning and a connecting bus for the wharf. Timetables, walking estimates and advertised travel times are deliberately not reproduced or guaranteed.</li><li>Evidence gaps: no building permission, property accessibility assessment, reserved parking, event-specific access plan, local roster, transport service, venue relationship or clinical sign-off verified. General contingency questions are editorial planning advice, not a claimed Gentle Care operating policy.</li><li>Locality identity: NSW Spatial Services gazetted Suburb layer, object ID 24217; https://portal.spatial.nsw.gov.au/server/rest/services/NSW_Administrative_Boundaries_Theme_multiCRS/FeatureServer/2. Postcode comes from the coverage register and must be checked for the actual address.</li><li>Coverage is owner-reported confirmed, recorded 2026-11-05; it is not independent verification of clinical scope or credentials.</li><li>Straight-line geographic inventory, not driving distance, travel time, an office-coordinate geocode, or a service availability guarantee. No office or travel time is inferred.</li><li>Shared content composition is not independent local research, approval or sufficient evidence for a separately indexed page.</li></ul></aside></div></details><div class=\"decision\"><strong>Decision for Sydney Olympic Park</strong><p>Copy: Yes / No / Changes needed &nbsp; \u00b7 &nbsp; Local facts: Confirmed / Needs checking &nbsp; \u00b7 &nbsp; Clinical and service review: Pending</p><p>Notes: _________________________________________________</p></div><a href=\"#top\">Back to overview</a></article><article id=\"lidcombe\"><header><p class=\"eyebrow\">NEXT BATCH \u00b7 REVIEW ONLY</p><h2>Lidcombe</h2><p class=\"status\">Council construction timing is published planning information, not proof of an actual closure. Station facilities in the original evidence were checked on 6 November, not refreshed here.</p></header><details open><summary>Read the full Lidcombe draft</summary><div class=\"draft\"><aside><strong>13 November evidence-only revision. NOT APPROVED FOR PUBLICATION.</strong> Source observations below are dated 12 November, not a fresh 13 November check. Clinical and service text is preserved but remains unapproved for this batch. PR79 approval does not apply.</aside><nav>Review index</nav><h2>Plan home nursing and care in Lidcombe</h2><aside>Unpublished draft for Gemma. Editing and evidence review are separate from release approval.</aside><p>Prepare a Lidcombe care enquiry with current access information, station travel considerations, clear care timing and a practical handover between the people involved.</p><p>When care is being arranged in Lidcombe, start with the person&#39;s daily needs and the home where help will happen. Family travel, local works and existing appointments may affect planning, but the proposed care tasks need their own assessment. A clear enquiry helps identify what must be agreed before a visit.</p><section><h2>Confirm the care location in Lidcombe</h2><p>The coverage register identifies Lidcombe, NSW, with recorded postcode 2141. Confirm the actual suburb and postcode for the address where care is needed, particularly when a relative, referrer or billing contact lives elsewhere. The recorded postcode is locality context, not verification of every street address.</p><p>Gentle Care&#39;s coverage for Lidcombe is owner-reported confirmed as recorded on 2026-11-05. The requested tasks, suitable staff, funding and visit times still need to be agreed for your enquiry. Coverage does not confirm an appointment or establish an office in this locality.</p></section><section><h2>Plan the proposed visit in Lidcombe</h2><p>Give the suburb, general care need and preferred start date first. Explain any essential visit times, current providers and whether a move or hospital discharge affects the timing. A proposed discharge or transition date does not reserve a visit.</p><p>Ask how the exact service address and access instructions should be shared securely. Discuss any stairs, shared entrance, equipment or privacy needs at the individual property; these are questions about your home, not assumptions about housing in the locality. Confirm any travel charges, cancellation terms and who to contact if plans change.</p><ul><li>Where will care actually take place, and who may help coordinate it?</li><li>What tasks and timing need to be assessed before a first visit?</li><li>Who holds the current instructions and can share them with consent?</li><li>What must be confirmed before you rely on a start date?</li></ul></section><section><h2>Check current access around the Joseph Street precinct</h2><p>Council\u2019s October 2026 Joseph Street precinct update warns of temporary construction disruption. If the home or a journey you rely on is near the precinct, check current Council information and any known changes to the actual entrance. The published construction timetable is not evidence of a particular closure, completed work or a step-free route to your home.</p><p>Tell the care team about any known change at the actual property, such as a relocated entrance or a delivery restriction. Confirm the street address and which entrance reaches the home. Ask how updated directions should be provided and who can be contacted if access differs on arrival. Avoid treating a proposed public-space improvement as evidence that a route is already step-free.</p></section><section><h2>Plan the connection from Lidcombe Station to the door</h2><p>Transport for NSW lists Lidcombe Station on Railway Parade with a lift, wheelchair ramp boarding assistance, hearing loop and an accessible toilet marked MLAK. These details may help a relative preparing to attend a care discussion. Check the facility you need, current alerts and the onward journey to the correct home entrance.</p><p>Transport for NSW&#39;s accessible-trip guidance explains how to filter for accessible services and check known lift outages. Agree a precise meeting location if someone is being met after travelling. A station facility listing does not confirm a suitable vehicle, an accessible path to a private home or a care appointment. Any requested escort or travel assistance should be discussed separately.</p></section><section><h2>Separate essential care timing from a preferred arrival</h2><p>List the general tasks you want help with and how often. Explain whether you are requesting nursing, everyday assistance or both, and which routines the person wants to manage independently. Where current care instructions set a timing requirement, identify it for the assessing team. Do not adjust that routine simply to fit a train service, a family member&#39;s workday or an assumed opening in the roster.</p><p>Say whether a relative must be present for consent, communication, entry or a handover. These are different roles and may need different arrangements. Ask what should happen if they are delayed, who receives the message and whether the visit can proceed. Describe communication preferences in the person&#39;s own terms and ask what support can be arranged before relying on it.</p></section><section><h2>Agree what changes and what existing support continues</h2><p>For a hospital discharge, provider change or temporary stay with family, identify the actual service address and proposed date. List who currently provides support and which tasks you want Gentle Care to consider. Ask who can supply current instructions, how consent will be confirmed and who is responsible for equipment, supplies and the period before the first accepted visit.</p><p>Explain the proposed NDIS, DVA, older-person care or private arrangement. Request confirmation of the tasks, suitable staffing, funding pathway and costs, including any travel or cancellation terms. Where an existing SIL team is involved, clarify their continuing responsibilities. A provider change should have an agreed handover; a preferred date or available funding alone does not confirm a start.</p></section><section><h2>Use the first enquiry to identify the decisions needed</h2><p>Begin with Lidcombe, the general support, the proposed start and a preferred contact method. Mention any known access issue or essential family attendance. Ask which information is needed next and who will confirm the service arrangements. Keep the person receiving care involved in choices about timing, communication and who may receive information.</p><p>Keep your first website message to your suburb, the general support you need and contact details. Ask how to share care plans, clinical instructions and property access information securely. Do not put health records, participant identifiers or door codes in the enquiry box.</p><p>This enquiry is for arranging planned support. If someone has a medical emergency or is in immediate danger, call 000 rather than waiting for a website reply.</p></section><h2>Referral preparation</h2><ul><li>Lidcombe service address and any current change to the usual property entrance.</li><li>General nursing or support tasks, frequency and essential timing separate from preferences.</li><li>People authorised to attend, their roles and any station-to-home journey questions.</li><li>Current providers, proposed transition date and the contact holding current instructions.</li><li>Equipment responsibilities, funding arrangement and questions about charges or delays.</li></ul><h2>Process</h2><ol><li><b>Explain the care request</b> Identify the tasks and routines that matter at the Lidcombe service address.</li><li><b>Check current access</b> Review the actual entrance, family attendance and known travel or works changes.</li><li><b>Settle the handover</b> Clarify instructions, equipment, provider roles, funding and written costs.</li><li><b>Confirm the arrangement</b> Agree the first visit and how delays or changes will be communicated.</li></ol><h2>Questions</h2><h3>Does coverage in Lidcombe guarantee a visit?</h3><p>No. Owner-confirmed coverage supports making an enquiry, but the team must confirm the proposed tasks, staffing, funding and visit arrangement for the individual person.</p><h3>Which location details should I give for care in Lidcombe?</h3><p>Start with Lidcombe and confirm the postcode for the care address; the register records 2141. Distinguish it from a family or billing address. Ask how to share the exact address and necessary access information securely.</p><h3>Does this page identify an office or travel time in Lidcombe?</h3><p>No. The locality inventory uses geographic boundaries and straight-line measurements. These do not establish an office, driving route, travel time or an appointment response time.</p><h3>Will Joseph Street works affect my care visit?</h3><p>Council warns of temporary disruption, but the effect on your particular address is not established here. Check current notices, report any known entrance changes and agree practical access with the team before relying on the usual arrangement.</p><h3>Is Lidcombe Station suitable for a relative using a wheelchair?</h3><p>Transport for NSW lists a lift and wheelchair ramp boarding assistance. Your relative should check current facilities, their travel needs and the route to the home. The listing does not guarantee every part of that journey will be suitable.</p><h3>Can a visit be booked around my relative&#39;s train?</h3><p>Explain the preferred time and why their attendance matters. The team must also consider the care requirements and staffing. Confirm both arrangements rather than treating a train arrival as a booked home visit, and discuss what happens if travel is delayed.</p><h3>Can nursing take over from our existing SIL support?</h3><p>Describe the tasks you want assessed and the support already provided. Visiting nursing and a SIL team&#39;s daily responsibilities need to be clarified separately. Do not assume a nursing enquiry replaces the existing provider or covers all hours of support.</p><h3>What should I prepare for a change of provider?</h3><p>Have a general summary of the requested tasks, current provider, proposed date and funding questions ready. Ask how to share instructions securely and agree responsibilities for equipment and any gap between services before changing the existing arrangement.</p><aside><h2>Review questions</h2><ul><li>Gemma: which Lidcombe nursing and support requests can be accepted, and who confirms task suitability?</li><li>Confirm the actual arrangements for changed entrances, family delays and essential visit timing.</li><li>Verify how clinical instructions, consent, equipment and existing SIL responsibilities are checked during a handover.</li><li>Confirm funding checks, written costs and the secure information-sharing route before publication.</li><li>Refresh the Joseph Street project and station evidence before publication; do not convert a works schedule into a verified closure or a station feature into whole-journey accessibility.</li><li>Gemma must review the actual service scope, referral process and capacity for the proposed arrangement.</li><li>Funding, clinical suitability and applicable credential scope require their own checks; no clinical sign-off is assigned by composition.</li><li>Distinct useful local evidence is not established by suburb/postcode substitution or shared prose. Resolve local value and canonical overlap before considering indexing.</li><li>Existing routes, metadata and technical QA require review and explicit release approval before publication.</li></ul><h2>Evidence</h2><ul><li>Owner-reported coverage: the registry records Tyson&#39;s report of Gemma&#39;s confirmation on 5 November 2026. This establishes locality planning scope only, not accepted care tasks, funding eligibility, an available appointment or clinical approval.</li><li>Registry identity checked 6 November 2026: Lidcombe, NSW 2141, nsw-19816; owner-reported-confirmed coverage; existingPath is null. Proposed path remains private draft metadata.</li><li>Read 6 November 2026: Cumberland City Council, Joseph Street Precinct Upgrade - October 2026, https://haveyoursay.cumberland.nsw.gov.au/joseph-street-precinct/news/joseph-street-precinct-upgrade-october-2026 . Warns of temporary construction disruption and lists commencement on 2 November 2026. The draft attributes that schedule rather than claiming independently observed commencement, a particular closure or a completed accessible route.</li><li>Read 6 November 2026: Transport for NSW, Lidcombe Station, https://transportnsw.info/stop?q=10101218 . Supports Railway Parade location, lift, wheelchair ramp boarding assistance, hearing loop and MLAK accessible toilet. Live facility status and the onward route remain to be checked.</li><li>Read 6 November 2026: Transport for NSW, Planning an accessible trip, https://transportnsw.info/travel-info/using-public-transport/accessible-travel/planning-accessible-trip . Supports accessible-service filters, facility checks, alerts and known lift-outage checks.</li><li>Evidence gaps: no clinical sign-off, suburb-specific roster, property assessment, current street closure, provider partnership or transport offer verified. Consumer copy describes questions to resolve before acceptance.</li><li>Locality identity: NSW Spatial Services gazetted Suburb layer, object ID 19816; https://portal.spatial.nsw.gov.au/server/rest/services/NSW_Administrative_Boundaries_Theme_multiCRS/FeatureServer/2. Postcode comes from the coverage register and must be checked for the actual address.</li><li>Coverage is owner-reported confirmed, recorded 2026-11-05; it is not independent verification of clinical scope or credentials.</li><li>Straight-line geographic inventory, not driving distance, travel time, an office-coordinate geocode, or a service availability guarantee. No office or travel time is inferred.</li><li>Shared content composition is not independent local research, approval or sufficient evidence for a separately indexed page.</li></ul></aside></div></details><div class=\"decision\"><strong>Decision for Lidcombe</strong><p>Copy: Yes / No / Changes needed &nbsp; \u00b7 &nbsp; Local facts: Confirmed / Needs checking &nbsp; \u00b7 &nbsp; Clinical and service review: Pending</p><p>Notes: _________________________________________________</p></div><a href=\"#top\">Back to overview</a></article><article id=\"newington\"><header><p class=\"eyebrow\">NEXT BATCH \u00b7 REVIEW ONLY</p><h2>Newington</h2><p class=\"status\">The event parking scheme was still described as proposed when checked on 12 November. Its decision timetable is stale. Community-centre facility evidence remains unrefreshed after a 403 response.</p></header><details open><summary>Read the full Newington draft</summary><div class=\"draft\"><aside><strong>13 November evidence-only revision. NOT APPROVED FOR PUBLICATION.</strong> Source observations below are dated 12 November, not a fresh 13 November check. Clinical and service text is preserved but remains unapproved for this batch. PR79 approval does not apply.</aside><nav>Review index</nav><h2>Arrange home nursing and everyday care in Newington</h2><aside>Unpublished draft for Gemma. Editing and evidence review are separate from release approval.</aside><p>Plan a Newington care enquiry around recurring routines, event-day access, home entry and clear responsibilities for support before and after community activities.</p><p>A useful care plan should support the parts of daily life you want to keep. In Newington, explain the help needed at home, regular commitments and any known access concerns. Gentle Care can consider the proposed support and the arrangements needed before tasks and visit times are agreed.</p><section><h2>Confirm the care location in Newington</h2><p>The coverage register identifies Newington, NSW, with recorded postcode 2127. Confirm the actual suburb and postcode for the address where care is needed, particularly when a relative, referrer or billing contact lives elsewhere. The recorded postcode is locality context, not verification of every street address.</p><p>Gentle Care&#39;s coverage for Newington is owner-reported confirmed as recorded on 2026-11-05. The requested tasks, suitable staff, funding and visit times still need to be agreed for your enquiry. Coverage does not confirm an appointment or establish an office in this locality.</p></section><section><h2>Plan the proposed visit in Newington</h2><p>Give the suburb, general care need and preferred start date first. Explain any essential visit times, current providers and whether a move or hospital discharge affects the timing. A proposed discharge or transition date does not reserve a visit.</p><p>Ask how the exact service address and access instructions should be shared securely. Discuss any stairs, shared entrance, equipment or privacy needs at the individual property; these are questions about your home, not assumptions about housing in the locality. Confirm any travel charges, cancellation terms and who to contact if plans change.</p><ul><li>Where will care actually take place, and who may help coordinate it?</li><li>What tasks and timing need to be assessed before a first visit?</li><li>Who holds the current instructions and can share them with consent?</li><li>What must be confirmed before you rely on a start date?</li></ul></section><section><h2>Discuss event-day access before relying on usual parking</h2><p>The Council page checked on 12 November 2026 still describes the Special Event Parking Scheme as proposed, while separately describing targeted parking restrictions introduced in 2025. Its anticipated decision period has passed; this does not establish that the proposal was approved. Check current Council information and signs at the location. Do not assume a care worker has a parking exemption or permission to use private spaces.</p><p>Describe known access difficulties and where an authorised visitor may enter. If someone must be home to admit a worker, coordinate their attendance and agree how delays will be communicated. Do not assume care workers have parking exemptions, permission to use a neighbour&#39;s space or guaranteed access to private streets.</p></section><section><h2>Build the enquiry around commitments that matter to you</h2><p>If you already attend activities at Newington Community Centre, give the activity time and describe the help you need around it. Council places the centre at the corner of Avenue of Europe and Avenue of Asia and lists wheelchair access and an accessible toilet. Check your particular activity, entry arrangements and required facilities directly with its organiser before relying on them.</p><p>Explain separately any assistance needed to prepare at home, travel, participate and return. A venue&#39;s access features do not establish that every activity or journey suits the person. Ask whether the proposed support can be considered and what assessment is required. Existing interests can guide a care discussion without making participation an obligation or assuming Gentle Care runs the activity.</p></section><section><h2>Include the return home in the support plan</h2><p>When family, an activity organiser or another provider is involved, agree where each person&#39;s responsibility begins and ends. Clarify who assists with the home entrance and what support is needed after the outing. A drop-off outside the property may not complete the help the person has requested. Describe the steps to the dwelling and any assistance you want assessed rather than assuming someone else will remain.</p><p>Discuss what happens if an activity finishes early, travel takes longer than expected or the person decides not to attend. Ask who should be contacted and how any changes to agreed visits are handled. Keep essential care timing separate from an optional outing. Questions about changing a clinical routine should go to the appropriate treating or assessing professional.</p></section><section><h2>Specify the care, instructions and funding being requested</h2><p>Describe the general nursing or everyday support tasks, the frequency and what is already being managed by the person or their existing team. If the person lives in a SIL home, distinguish the house team&#39;s responsibilities from the visits being requested. Ask which tasks could be accepted, what current instructions are needed and who will confirm the required equipment or supplies.</p><p>For an NDIS, DVA, older-person care or private arrangement, check the particular service and payment pathway. Discuss written costs, including travel and cancellation terms, and whether outing-related assistance is a separate service. If a provider change or hospital discharge is involved, agree the handover and first visit before treating the new support as confirmed. An event calendar or community booking cannot reserve staff.</p></section><section><h2>Send a short enquiry with your main priorities</h2><p>Start with Newington, the general support and your preferred contact method. Mention a proposed start, recurring commitment or property access issue. Explain who you want involved and what they may discuss. Ask what needs checking next so the person receiving care can take part in decisions.</p><p>Keep your first website message to your suburb, the general support you need and contact details. Ask how to share care plans, clinical instructions and property access information securely. Do not put health records, participant identifiers or door codes in the enquiry box.</p><p>This enquiry is for arranging planned support. If someone has a medical emergency or is in immediate danger, call 000 rather than waiting for a website reply.</p></section><h2>Referral preparation</h2><ul><li>Newington service location and known visitor-entry or event-day access concerns.</li><li>General tasks, frequency, preferred visit windows and any essential care timing.</li><li>Regular commitments the person wants to keep and the help requested before and afterwards.</li><li>Current support roles, authorised contacts and responsibility for the return home.</li><li>Funding questions, equipment or supply needs and any proposed provider transition.</li></ul><h2>Process</h2><ol><li><b>Explain the daily priorities</b> Describe the care needed and routines the person wants to preserve in Newington.</li><li><b>Review access and transitions</b> Clarify home entry, attendance, outings and who remains responsible at each stage.</li><li><b>Check the proposed services</b> Confirm suitability, instructions, staff requirements, funding and written costs.</li><li><b>Agree the visit plan</b> Set the first visit and communication arrangements for changed plans or access delays.</li></ol><h2>Questions</h2><h3>Does coverage in Newington guarantee a visit?</h3><p>No. Owner-confirmed coverage supports making an enquiry, but the team must confirm the proposed tasks, staffing, funding and visit arrangement for the individual person.</p><h3>Which location details should I give for care in Newington?</h3><p>Start with Newington and confirm the postcode for the care address; the register records 2127. Distinguish it from a family or billing address. Ask how to share the exact address and necessary access information securely.</p><h3>Does this page identify an office or travel time in Newington?</h3><p>No. The locality inventory uses geographic boundaries and straight-line measurements. These do not establish an office, driving route, travel time or an appointment response time.</p><h3>Is the proposed Newington event parking scheme already operating?</h3><p>The Council page checked on 6 November 2026 still describes it as a proposal. Recheck current Council information and signs before a visit. This page does not establish that a permit is available, required or held by a care worker.</p><h3>Can care be planned around my community centre activity?</h3><p>Explain the activity time, its importance to you and what help you need before or after it. The team must check care timing, suitable staffing and the requested service. The activity booking itself does not confirm a care appointment.</p><h3>Does support include getting me back inside my home?</h3><p>Describe that need explicitly, including the entrance and any assistance required. Agree where responsibilities end and who will remain if further help is needed. Do not assume a transport drop-off or another provider&#39;s involvement includes the complete return-home task.</p><h3>What happens if I cancel an outing but still need care?</h3><p>Ask how to notify the team and which services can continue under the agreed arrangement. Clarify any cancellation terms before starting. A change to an optional activity should not be treated as permission to change essential care instructions without appropriate advice.</p><h3>Can family make the first enquiry for me?</h3><p>A family member can help describe the general request. Explain how you want to participate, who may discuss information and what authority applies. Detailed care records and home access information should be shared only through the agreed secure process.</p><aside><h2>Review questions</h2><ul><li>Gemma: which Newington care tasks and visit patterns can be accepted after assessment?</li><li>Confirm the practical procedure for event-day access issues, delayed entry and changes to planned visits.</li><li>Verify the scope of outing assistance, transport and return-home support without implying a bundled service.</li><li>Confirm consent, funding checks, written fees, cancellation terms and handover responsibilities with existing providers.</li><li>Recheck the Newington parking proposal and community centre facilities before publication; do not claim the scheme is implemented, a permit exemption exists or a venue partnership is established.</li><li>Gemma must review the actual service scope, referral process and capacity for the proposed arrangement.</li><li>Funding, clinical suitability and applicable credential scope require their own checks; no clinical sign-off is assigned by composition.</li><li>Distinct useful local evidence is not established by suburb/postcode substitution or shared prose. Resolve local value and canonical overlap before considering indexing.</li><li>Existing routes, metadata and technical QA require review and explicit release approval before publication.</li></ul><h2>Evidence</h2><ul><li>Owner-reported coverage: the registry records Tyson&#39;s report of Gemma&#39;s confirmation on 5 November 2026. This establishes locality planning scope only, not accepted care tasks, funding eligibility, an available appointment or clinical approval.</li><li>Registry identity checked 6 November 2026: Newington, NSW 2127, nsw-26564; owner-reported-confirmed coverage; existingPath is null. Proposed path is private draft metadata only.</li><li>Read 6 November 2026: City of Parramatta, Newington Parking Restrictions, https://participate.cityofparramatta.nsw.gov.au/newington-parking-restrictions . Supports event-related parking pressure and describes the Special Event Parking Scheme as proposed. Implementation, permits, fees and activation dates are not asserted; the source&#39;s projected decision dates may be stale and require rechecking before publication.</li><li>Read 6 November 2026: City of Parramatta, Newington Community Centre, https://www.cityofparramatta.nsw.gov.au/community/places-for-hire/community-centres-and-event-spaces/newington-community-centre . Supports the corner of Avenue of Europe and Avenue of Asia location, wheelchair accessibility and accessible toilet listing. Individual activities, journey suitability and Gentle Care involvement are not established.</li><li>Evidence gaps: no clinical sign-off, locality-specific staffing, property assessment, transport offer, event parking entitlement or community-centre partnership verified. Planning prompts require individual agreement rather than promising an operating process.</li><li>Locality identity: NSW Spatial Services gazetted Suburb layer, object ID 26564; https://portal.spatial.nsw.gov.au/server/rest/services/NSW_Administrative_Boundaries_Theme_multiCRS/FeatureServer/2. Postcode comes from the coverage register and must be checked for the actual address.</li><li>Coverage is owner-reported confirmed, recorded 2026-11-05; it is not independent verification of clinical scope or credentials.</li><li>Straight-line geographic inventory, not driving distance, travel time, an office-coordinate geocode, or a service availability guarantee. No office or travel time is inferred.</li><li>Shared content composition is not independent local research, approval or sufficient evidence for a separately indexed page.</li></ul></aside></div></details><div class=\"decision\"><strong>Decision for Newington</strong><p>Copy: Yes / No / Changes needed &nbsp; \u00b7 &nbsp; Local facts: Confirmed / Needs checking &nbsp; \u00b7 &nbsp; Clinical and service review: Pending</p><p>Notes: _________________________________________________</p></div><a href=\"#top\">Back to overview</a></article><article id=\"wentworth-point\"><header><p class=\"eyebrow\">NEXT BATCH \u00b7 REVIEW ONLY</p><h2>Wentworth Point</h2><p class=\"status\">Original draft, unchanged. Council venue and journey information was last verified on 6 November. Refresh failed with a 403 response on 12 November; venue accessibility and connections are not newly verified.</p></header><details open><summary>Read the full Wentworth Point draft</summary><div class=\"draft\"><nav>Review index</nav><h2>Arrange home nursing and support in Wentworth Point</h2><aside>Unpublished draft for Gemma. Editing and evidence review are separate from release approval.</aside><p>Prepare a Wentworth Point care enquiry around your home entrance, family attendance, community routines and the responsibilities needed for a workable first visit.</p><p>Care at home needs to fit the person as well as the address. For a Wentworth Point enquiry, explain the help you need, how a visitor would reach your home and which routines you want to keep. Gentle Care can consider the proposed tasks and practical arrangements before a service is agreed.</p><section><h2>Confirm the care location in Wentworth Point</h2><p>The coverage register identifies Wentworth Point, NSW, with recorded postcode 2127. Confirm the actual suburb and postcode for the address where care is needed, particularly when a relative, referrer or billing contact lives elsewhere. The recorded postcode is locality context, not verification of every street address.</p><p>Gentle Care&#39;s coverage for Wentworth Point is owner-reported confirmed as recorded on 2026-11-05. The requested tasks, suitable staff, funding and visit times still need to be agreed for your enquiry. Coverage does not confirm an appointment or establish an office in this locality.</p></section><section><h2>Plan the proposed visit in Wentworth Point</h2><p>Give the suburb, general care need and preferred start date first. Explain any essential visit times, current providers and whether a move or hospital discharge affects the timing. A proposed discharge or transition date does not reserve a visit.</p><p>Ask how the exact service address and access instructions should be shared securely. Discuss any stairs, shared entrance, equipment or privacy needs at the individual property; these are questions about your home, not assumptions about housing in the locality. Confirm any travel charges, cancellation terms and who to contact if plans change.</p><ul><li>Where will care actually take place, and who may help coordinate it?</li><li>What tasks and timing need to be assessed before a first visit?</li><li>Who holds the current instructions and can share them with consent?</li><li>What must be confirmed before you rely on a start date?</li></ul></section><section><h2>Identify the home entrance, not just a familiar landmark</h2><p>A reference to the waterfront, a shopping centre or the community library can help someone recognise the area, but the service address needs to identify the actual dwelling. Confirm Wentworth Point, the street and building during intake. If your home has more than one entrance, describe which one reaches the dwelling and whether a visitor needs to use an intercom, lift or stairs.</p><p>Ask who will open the building and the home, and what should happen if that person is unavailable. Where building management controls visitor access or equipment deliveries, check the arrangements that apply to your property. Do not assume a visiting worker has permission to use a resident&#39;s parking space or enter a secured area. Share detailed access instructions only through the agreed secure route.</p></section><section><h2>Coordinate family attendance with the whole journey</h2><p>Council&#39;s directions to Wentworth Point Community Centre and Library describe connections from Rhodes, a walking route across Bennelong Bridge and Sydney Olympic Park Wharf as the nearest ferry wharf to the centre. These can help a relative understand local travel options. Plan their journey to your actual home rather than using a public venue as the destination.</p><p>If a relative is needed for the first discussion, explain their role and confirm your permission for them to participate. Check the current journey and any accessibility needs before agreeing attendance. Allow for the steps between arriving in the neighbourhood and reaching your door. Ask who should be contacted if the relative is delayed and whether their presence is essential for the visit to proceed.</p></section><section><h2>Describe the community routines you want to preserve</h2><p>If the library is part of your week, include it when explaining your goals. Council describes Wentworth Point Community Centre and Library as wheelchair accessible, with hearing augmentation. Check any particular facility or activity you rely on directly with the venue. Its published features do not establish the suitability of the journey or the accessibility of your home.</p><p>Separate help getting ready at home from support during an outing, transport and assistance after returning. Say which parts you manage yourself and which you want assessed. A nursing visit does not automatically include an escort or transport. Agree who remains responsible during each part of the day, especially if family and another provider already share the support.</p></section><section><h2>Make the proposed visits and handover clear</h2><p>Describe the requested nursing or everyday support, frequency and preferred visit windows. Identify any timing requirements in current care instructions separately from a convenient family arrival time. Ask what assessment, skills, equipment and written information are required for the particular tasks. If the person is returning from hospital or changing providers, say who can provide the current instructions with consent.</p><p>For NDIS, DVA, older-person care or a private arrangement, ask the team to check the proposed service and payment pathway. Clarify charges, supplies, travel and cancellation terms before agreeing. Confirm the first visit separately from a discharge date, family travel plan or building access booking. Keep responsibility for existing support clear until a changeover has been accepted.</p></section><section><h2>Start with enough detail for a useful conversation</h2><p>Begin with Wentworth Point, the general support and a preferred way to reply. Mention an access concern, proposed start date or community commitment if it affects the arrangement. Explain how the person wants to take part in planning and any communication support they would like considered. Ask what must be settled before a visit can be confirmed.</p><p>Keep your first website message to your suburb, the general support you need and contact details. Ask how to share care plans, clinical instructions and property access information securely. Do not put health records, participant identifiers or door codes in the enquiry box.</p><p>This enquiry is for arranging planned support. If someone has a medical emergency or is in immediate danger, call 000 rather than waiting for a website reply.</p></section><h2>Referral preparation</h2><ul><li>Wentworth Point service location, with the building and dwelling entrance to confirm during intake.</li><li>Requested tasks, visit frequency, preferred routine and any essential care timing.</li><li>Authorised family participants, their role and whether attendance depends on public transport.</li><li>Home entry, lift or stair questions and responsibility for equipment or supplies.</li><li>Existing providers, community commitments, proposed changeover and funding questions.</li></ul><h2>Process</h2><ol><li><b>Describe life at home</b> Explain the tasks, routines and support already in place in Wentworth Point.</li><li><b>Clarify access and attendance</b> Confirm the entrance, authorised participants and arrangements if someone is delayed.</li><li><b>Review the proposed support</b> Check suitability, instructions, equipment, funding and charges for each requested service.</li><li><b>Agree the first visit</b> Confirm responsibilities, timing and the communication plan before care begins.</li></ol><h2>Questions</h2><h3>Does coverage in Wentworth Point guarantee a visit?</h3><p>No. Owner-confirmed coverage supports making an enquiry, but the team must confirm the proposed tasks, staffing, funding and visit arrangement for the individual person.</p><h3>Which location details should I give for care in Wentworth Point?</h3><p>Start with Wentworth Point and confirm the postcode for the care address; the register records 2127. Distinguish it from a family or billing address. Ask how to share the exact address and necessary access information securely.</p><h3>Does this page identify an office or travel time in Wentworth Point?</h3><p>No. The locality inventory uses geographic boundaries and straight-line measurements. These do not establish an office, driving route, travel time or an appointment response time.</p><h3>Does coverage mean a visit is already available?</h3><p>Wentworth Point is within the confirmed geographic coverage. The team still needs to assess your requested support and confirm suitable staffing, funding, access and a visit time. An enquiry or preferred start date does not reserve an appointment.</p><h3>Can my relative meet the worker at the library?</h3><p>Explain where you want the care discussion to happen and why. A public venue should not be assumed to provide a private or suitable setting for health information. Agree the meeting location and your relative&#39;s involvement with the team first.</p><h3>Does library accessibility mean my whole outing is accessible?</h3><p>The venue lists accessibility features, but the route, transport, your individual needs and return arrangements still need checking. Describe the assistance you want, including any help at home before or after the outing.</p><h3>Can a worker use visitor parking inside my building?</h3><p>Check the actual property&#39;s conditions and who can authorise entry. Tell the team about any visitor booking or access requirement during planning. Do not assume a space is available or that permission to enter one area covers the whole building.</p><h3>Can transport and nursing be organised as one request?</h3><p>You can describe both needs in your enquiry. They need separate checks for scope, suitability, responsibilities and payment. Confirm the services actually accepted rather than assuming travel assistance is included with a nursing visit.</p><aside><h2>Review questions</h2><ul><li>Gemma: which Wentworth Point nursing and everyday support requests can be accepted after assessment?</li><li>Confirm how property access, equipment deliveries and authorised keyholders are checked without assuming private-space permission.</li><li>What happens when an essential family participant is delayed or cannot attend?</li><li>Verify the scope and funding checks for outings, transport and home visits, including written charges and secure handover.</li><li>Recheck Council links before publication; retain venue information as planning context without promising accessible journeys or a Council partnership.</li><li>Gemma must review the actual service scope, referral process and capacity for the proposed arrangement.</li><li>Funding, clinical suitability and applicable credential scope require their own checks; no clinical sign-off is assigned by composition.</li><li>Distinct useful local evidence is not established by suburb/postcode substitution or shared prose. Resolve local value and canonical overlap before considering indexing.</li><li>Existing routes, metadata and technical QA require review and explicit release approval before publication.</li></ul><h2>Evidence</h2><ul><li>Owner-reported coverage: the registry records Tyson&#39;s report of Gemma&#39;s confirmation on 5 November 2026. This establishes locality planning scope only, not accepted care tasks, funding eligibility, an available appointment or clinical approval.</li><li>Registry identity checked 6 November 2026: Wentworth Point, NSW 2127, nsw-29012; owner-reported-confirmed coverage; existingPath is null. The proposed path is private draft metadata only.</li><li>Read 6 November 2026: City of Parramatta, Getting to Wentworth Point Community Centre and Library, https://www.cityofparramatta.nsw.gov.au/community/libraries-and-community-hubs/community-hub-locations-and-opening-hours/wentworth-point-community-centre-and-library/getting-to-wentworth-point-community-centre-and-library . Supports Rhodes travel connections, a walking route across Bennelong Bridge and Sydney Olympic Park Wharf as nearest to the centre. No travel times, shuttle frequency, parking entitlement or home-access claims are adopted.</li><li>Read 6 November 2026: City of Parramatta, Wentworth Point Community Centre and Library, https://www.cityofparramatta.nsw.gov.au/community/libraries-and-community-hubs/community-hub-locations-and-opening-hours/wentworth-point-community-centre-and-library . Supports the venue&#39;s wheelchair accessibility and hearing augmentation; does not establish individual suitability or any Gentle Care relationship.</li><li>Evidence gaps: no clinical sign-off, property assessment, locality-specific roster, transport service, venue partnership or confirmed appointment verified. Access and coordination copy describes matters to agree, not established provider procedures.</li><li>Locality identity: NSW Spatial Services gazetted Suburb layer, object ID 29012; https://portal.spatial.nsw.gov.au/server/rest/services/NSW_Administrative_Boundaries_Theme_multiCRS/FeatureServer/2. Postcode comes from the coverage register and must be checked for the actual address.</li><li>Coverage is owner-reported confirmed, recorded 2026-11-05; it is not independent verification of clinical scope or credentials.</li><li>Straight-line geographic inventory, not driving distance, travel time, an office-coordinate geocode, or a service availability guarantee. No office or travel time is inferred.</li><li>Shared content composition is not independent local research, approval or sufficient evidence for a separately indexed page.</li></ul></aside></div></details><div class=\"decision\"><strong>Decision for Wentworth Point</strong><p>Copy: Yes / No / Changes needed &nbsp; \u00b7 &nbsp; Local facts: Confirmed / Needs checking &nbsp; \u00b7 &nbsp; Clinical and service review: Pending</p><p>Notes: _________________________________________________</p></div><a href=\"#top\">Back to overview</a></article></main></body></html>";
